window.__labLoaded({"id":"8.1","title":"Correlation: Do Two Numbers Move Together?","goal":"Measure how strongly study hours and exam scores move together for the lesson's eight students, with Pearson's r, Spearman's rho and a scatter plot.","question":"A teacher wonders whether studying more goes with higher exam scores. Eight students reported the hours they studied in the week before the exam (1 to 8 hours) and then their exam score. How strongly do hours and score move together, and with only eight students, could the pattern be luck?","python":{"blocks":[{"title":"Enter the data and look at it","text":"Eight students: hours studied and exam score. Always draw the scatter plot before trusting a correlation number, because the plot shows the pattern, outliers and curves that one number hides.","code":"import numpy as np\nimport matplotlib.pyplot as plt\nfrom scipy import stats\n\nhours = np.array([1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8])\nscore = np.array([48, 62, 55, 66, 63, 74, 70, 82])\n\nplt.figure(figsize=(6, 3.4))\nplt.scatter(hours, score, color='#1f77b4')\nplt.xlabel('Hours studied')\nplt.ylabel('Exam score')\nplt.title('Study hours and exam score')\nplt.show()","explain":["Load NumPy, the library for arrays of numbers, under the short name np.","Load matplotlib's plotting tools as plt, so we can draw the scatter plot.","Load SciPy's statistics tools, which contain the correlation functions.","Store the hours studied by the eight students as an array, one number per student.","Store their exam scores in the same order, so the first score belongs to the first hours value.","Start a new figure. figsize=(6, 3.4) sets its width and height in inches so it fits the page.","Draw one dot per student, hours across and score up. Always plot first: one number can hide curves and outliers.","Label the horizontal axis so a reader knows what it shows.","Label the vertical axis too.","Add a title that says in words what the plot is about.","Show the figure."],"meaning":"The dots climb from lower left to upper right in a fairly straight band. Students who studied more tended to score more. The 3-hour student (55) scored below the 2-hour student (62), so it is not perfect. There is no wild outlier, so a straight-line measure like Pearson's r is a fair choice.","out":"","figs":["8.1-1.png"]},{"title":"Pearson's r","text":"pearsonr measures the strength of a straight-line link from -1 to +1. It also returns a p-value for the test that the true correlation is 0, which uses the t distribution with n - 2 degrees of freedom.","code":"res = stats.pearsonr(hours, score)\n\nprint(f'r       = {res.statistic:.3f}')\nprint(f'r squared = {res.statistic ** 2:.3f}')\nprint(f'p-value = {res.pvalue:.4f}')\nprint('df      =', len(hours) - 2)","explain":["Pearson's correlation: pearsonr returns r (strength of the straight-line link) and a p-value for 'is the true correlation 0?'.","Print r, from -1 (perfect downhill) through 0 (no straight-line link) to +1 (perfect uphill).","Squaring r gives the share of the variation in scores that moves together with hours.","The p-value says how rare an r this far from 0 would be if the true correlation were 0.","Degrees of freedom for this test are n - 2. We print them because the t-test behind the p-value uses them."],"meaning":"r = 0.907 is a strong positive link. r squared = 0.822, so about 82% of the ups and downs in score go along with hours. The p-value is 0.0019 (6 df): if hours and scores were truly unrelated, an r this large from 8 students would be very unusual. So luck is an unlikely explanation.","out":"r       = 0.907\nr squared = 0.822\np-value = 0.0019\ndf      = 6","figs":[]},{"title":"Spearman's rho (the rank version)","text":"Spearman replaces each value by its rank and then correlates the ranks. It catches any steadily rising or falling pattern, curved or not, and is less bothered by outliers.","code":"sp = stats.spearmanr(hours, score)\n\nprint(f'rho     = {sp.statistic:.3f}')\nprint(f'p-value = {sp.pvalue:.4f}')","explain":["Spearman's rho: spearmanr replaces every value by its rank and correlates the ranks, so it catches any steady rise or fall.","Print rho. It is also between -1 and +1 and is less bothered by outliers.","Print its p-value, the chance of a rho this far from 0 if there were no link at all."],"meaning":"rho = 0.929 is even higher than r, because the ranks are almost perfectly in step. The p-value of 0.0009 is an approximation (R reports an exact 0.0022). Since the rank version agrees with Pearson's, the result does not depend on one odd student.","out":"rho     = 0.929\np-value = 0.0009","figs":[]},{"title":"Correlation is not causation","text":"A strong r tells us hours and score rise together. It cannot tell us that studying causes the higher scores: keen, well-prepared students may both study more and score higher. Only a well-designed experiment can settle cause.","code":"print(f'r = {res.statistic:.2f}: the two variables rise together.')\nprint('That does NOT prove that extra hours caused extra points.')\nprint('Something else (motivation, prior skill) could drive both.')","explain":["Print r rounded to 2 decimals and say in words what it shows: the two variables rise together.","Remind ourselves that a strong correlation does not prove that studying caused the higher scores.","Name a possible hidden driver. Motivation or prior skill could lift both hours and scores."],"meaning":"r = 0.91 tells us the pattern is strong, not why it happens. Perhaps studying raises scores, or perhaps keen, well-prepared students both study more and score higher. A correlation alone cannot separate these. Only a well-designed experiment can.","out":"r = 0.91: the two variables rise together.\nThat does NOT prove that extra hours caused extra points.\nSomething else (motivation, prior skill) could drive both.","figs":[]}]},"r":{"code":"hours <- c(1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8)\nscore <- c(48, 62, 55, 66, 63, 74, 70, 82)\nplot(hours, score)\ncor.test(hours, score)\ncor.test(hours, score, method = \"spearman\")","expect":"t = 5.267, df = 6, p-value = 0.001889; 95 percent CI 0.5597 to 0.9832; cor 0.9067; Spearman S = 6, p-value = 0.002232, rho 0.9286","note":"cor.test gives Pearson's r by default and also a confidence interval. For Spearman with 8 values R reports an exact p-value, (0.0022), while Python and SPSS use an approximate p-value (0.0009); rho itself is identical.","explain":["Store the hours studied by the eight students as a vector. c() means combine.","Store their scores in the same order.","Draw the scatter plot of score against hours. Always look at the picture before trusting a number.","cor.test gives Pearson's r by default, with a t-test, a p-value and a 95% confidence interval.","method = \"spearman\" ranks the values first and gives Spearman's rho and its p-value."],"read":"In the first test find cor 0.9067, t = 5.267, df = 6, p-value = 0.001889 and the 95 percent interval 0.5597 to 0.9832: a strong link unlikely to be luck. In the second, rho 0.9286 and p-value = 0.002232 (an exact p, so larger than Python's approximate 0.0009)."},"spss":{"menu":"Analyze > Correlate > Bivariate (tick Pearson and Spearman); Graphs > Chart Builder > Scatter/Dot","syntax":"DATA LIST FREE / hours score.\nBEGIN DATA\n1 48  2 62  3 55  4 66  5 63  6 74  7 70  8 82\nEND DATA.\nGRAPH /SCATTERPLOT(BIVAR)=hours WITH score.\nCORRELATIONS /VARIABLES=hours score.\nNONPAR CORR /VARIABLES=hours score /PRINT=SPEARMAN TWOTAIL.","note":"CORRELATIONS gives Pearson's r with its two-tailed Sig.; NONPAR CORR with SPEARMAN gives rho. SPSS and Stata also use an approximate p-value for Spearman (about 0.001), so it is smaller than R's exact 0.0022.","explain":["Declare the two variables to read in free format: hours and score.","Marks the start of the data typed into the syntax.","Eight students, each as an hours value then a score.","Marks the end of the data.","Draw a scatter plot of hours against score. Looking at the plot first is good habit.","Pearson correlation between hours and score, with its two-tailed p-value.","NONPAR CORR with SPEARMAN gives Spearman's rho; TWOTAIL asks for the two-tailed p-value."],"read":"In the Correlations table read Pearson Correlation = .907 and Sig. (2-tailed) = .002: a strong link, unlikely by chance. In the Nonparametric Correlations table read Correlation Coefficient = .929 (rho) and Sig. (2-tailed) about .001."},"stata":{"code":"clear\nset obs 8\ngenerate hours = _n\ngenerate score = real(word(\"48 62 55 66 63 74 70 82\", _n))\nscatter score hours\npwcorr hours score, sig\nspearman hours score","note":"pwcorr prints Pearson's r with its p-value (sig); spearman prints rho and its p-value. scatter draws the plot.","explain":["Clear any old data from memory.","Create eight empty rows, one per student.","Make hours equal to the row number (_n), which gives 1 to 8.","Fill the score column. word() picks the n-th number from the quoted list and real() turns that text into a number.","Draw a scatter plot of score against hours.","pwcorr prints Pearson's r; sig adds its p-value.","spearman prints Spearman's rho and its p-value."],"read":"pwcorr shows 0.9067 with 0.0019 under it: r and its p-value, a strong link unlikely to be luck. spearman shows Number of obs = 8, Spearman's rho = 0.9286 and Prob > |t| = 0.0009 (an approximation)."},"conclusion":"Yes, in these eight students more study hours went with higher scores, and the link is strong: r = 0.91 (about 82% of the variation moves together), with p = 0.0019, so it is unlikely to be luck. Caution: this does not show that studying causes higher scores, and eight students from one class is a small sample.","practice":{"scenario":"A cafe owner records the day's high temperature (degrees Celsius) and the number of ice-cream cones sold on ten days. How strongly are temperature and sales linked, and does that prove heat makes people buy ice cream?","data":"temp = [14, 16, 18, 20, 22, 24, 26, 28, 30, 32]\nsales = [210, 250, 235, 300, 320, 310, 380, 400, 390, 450]","tasks":["Compute Pearson's r, its p-value and r squared.","Compute Spearman's rho and compare it with r.","In one sentence, say what you can and cannot conclude."],"hint":"Use stats.pearsonr and stats.spearmanr on the two lists. Square r to get the share of variation that moves together.","solution":{"code":"import numpy as np\nfrom scipy import stats\n\ntemp = np.array([14, 16, 18, 20, 22, 24, 26, 28, 30, 32])\nsales = np.array([210, 250, 235, 300, 320, 310, 380, 400, 390, 450])\nr = stats.pearsonr(temp, sales)\nrho = stats.spearmanr(temp, sales)\nprint(f'r = {r.statistic:.3f}, p = {r.pvalue:.1e}, r squared = {r.statistic ** 2:.3f}')\nprint(f'rho = {rho.statistic:.3f}, p = {rho.pvalue:.1e}')","answer":"r = 0.973 with p = 2.2e-06 (0.0000022) and r squared = 0.947, so about 95% of the variation in sales moves together with temperature. Spearman's rho = 0.964 (p = 7.3e-06) agrees. Hot days clearly go with more sales. But this is correlation: it does not prove heat alone causes the sales, since hot days also bring more people outdoors.","out":"r = 0.973, p = 2.2e-06, r squared = 0.947\nrho = 0.964, p = 7.3e-06","figs":[]}}});
